## 3️⃣ Why loading secrets from plain‑text `.env` files is a problem in production  

| Issue | Why it matters |
|-------|----------------|
| **File‑system exposure** – Any user (or compromised container) that can read the filesystem can dump the file and obtain all secrets. |
| **Accidental commit** – `.env` files are often mistakenly added to source control, leaking credentials to the public (or to any teammate that shouldn’t see them). |
| **Lack of rotation & audit** – Secrets stored on disk are not centrally versioned or audited; rotating them requires a manual file change on every host. |
| **Environment‑specific leakage** – In CI pipelines a stray `.env` can be printed in logs, creating a supply‑chain risk. |
| **Static credentials** – They are not scoped to the host or service, so a breach of one server gives an attacker a “master key”. |

